WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.420
Fill and sign PDFs without uploading them.

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.392
Form fields, signature images and certificate signatures explained, plus

3
00:00:11.392 --> 00:00:16.864
the order I fill, sign and flatten documents in, all inside the browser.

4
00:00:18.264 --> 00:00:20.964
Two very different things called signing.

5
00:00:20.964 --> 00:00:25.048
Before the how, one distinction that confuses a lot of people.

6
00:00:26.348 --> 00:00:32.293
Filling in a form. Many PDFs from banks, schools and government offices

7
00:00:32.293 --> 00:00:38.572
have real form fields: text boxes, checkboxes, dropdowns and radio buttons.

8
00:00:39.872 --> 00:00:45.282
Signing the page. Sign PDF gives you three ways to make a signature: draw

9
00:00:45.282 --> 00:00:50.691
it, type your name in a handwriting style font, or upload a photo of your

10
00:00:50.691 --> 00:00:52.544
signature on white paper.

11
00:00:53.844 --> 00:00:57.600
Certificate signatures without uploading your key.

12
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:03.459
If you have a personal or company certificate, Digital Signature (Certificate)

13
00:01:03.459 --> 00:01:07.967
opens your .p12 or .pfx file locally, asks for its password.

14
00:01:09.267 --> 00:01:12.201
To try it, open Sign PDF in OpenFile.

15
00:01:12.201 --> 00:01:16.721
Drop in a file, adjust the settings and press the button.

16
00:01:16.721 --> 00:01:20.765
It all runs on your device, so nothing is uploaded.

17
00:01:22.465 --> 00:01:26.753
Read the full post, with live demos, on the OpenFile blog.
